// Sentence-level publication decisions. Evidence is recorded in the publication ledger.
module.exports = {
  91: { hook: "Helen Toner's [current CSET biography](https://cset.georgetown.edu/staff/helen-toner/) identifies her as executive director, without the supplied interim qualifier. Her work connects policy debates with research on AI capability and access.", career: "The [institutional biography](https://cset.georgetown.edu/staff/helen-toner/) documents her security-studies and engineering background and earlier policy work. I use that current role record instead of inferring it from an older appointment announcement. I leave out an unverified birth year, precise early-employment dates and subscriber counts that do not establish the quality of the policy research." },
  92: { hook: "Sarah Guo's [Conviction launch statement](https://www.conviction.com/launching-with-conviction) describes a $100 million first fund built around AI-first companies. The original statement corrects the supplied $101 million amount.", education: "I could not confirm the supplied complete degree list and award dates against a primary university record.", career: "The launch statement is signed by Guo and lays out the firm's foundation-model investment thesis. [January 2025 reporting](https://techcrunch.com/2025/01/31/guos-conviction-partners-adds-mike-vernal-as-gp-raises-230m-fund) describes a later $230 million fund. I keep capital raised for a fund separate from portfolio-company valuations and omit uncertain ages, individual first-check claims and a later fund target not established by an original closing record.", outlook: "I am watching whether the investment thesis produces durable companies after model access and inference costs change. A venture fund's size measures capital available to invest, not realized returns. Portfolio markups are different from cash returned to investors, and either can look strong before the underlying businesses prove durable." },
  93: { hook: "Nathan Benaich's [State of AI site](https://www.stateof.ai/) presents the ninth annual report in 2026. The report is useful to follow because its prior predictions can be compared with later evidence.", education: "[Gates Cambridge's biography](https://www.gatescambridge.org/biography/6331/) documents his Cambridge scholarship and biological-science studies. I have not confirmed every supplied degree award year.", career: "The report's first-person introduction describes cancer and computational-biology doctoral research in the early 2010s and investing in AI companies since 2013. His Air Street role connects a research synthesis with an investment perspective. I omit an unverified complete portfolio list and a precise founding chronology when the report itself is the direct record being assessed.", outlook: "[March 2026 reporting](https://tech.eu/2026/03/23/nathan-benaich-s-air-street-raises-232m-fund-iii-becoming-europe-s-largest-solo-gp-venture-firm/) describes Air Street's $232 million Fund III. I am watching how the next report scores earlier predictions, including those that failed. The investment position should remain visible when readers judge a report's conclusions about technologies and companies." },
  94: { hook: "Leigh Marie Braswell's [Kleiner Perkins biography](https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/leigh-marie-braswell/) connects early engineering and product work at Scale AI with investing in infrastructure and AI applications.", career: "The firm identifies her as a partner, dates her Kleiner move to 2023 after Founders Fund and describes earlier work on Scale's 3D annotation product. It lists Avoca and Poetic board roles and investments including Windsurf, Neon and Chronosphere. I attribute the portfolio to the firm's account and leave out uncertain personal first-check claims or realized proceeds; an acquisition involving a portfolio company is not automatically a disclosed investor return.", outlook: "I am watching whether infrastructure and agent companies turn growing adoption into retained customers and a durable business. A private-company price can rise while the unit economics remain unresolved. Product experience offers a useful perspective on that gap, but an investor biography is not an independent performance audit." },
  95: { hook: "Grace Isford's [Lux biography](https://www.luxcapital.com/people/grace-isford) describes investments at the intersection of computation, software and the physical world. Its first-check and early-partner wording is the firm's account, not proof she individually led every listed deal.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied precise degree award year against an original Stanford record.", career: "Lux identifies her as a New York-based partner after Canvas Ventures, product work at Handshake and work at the Stanford Endowment. It lists companies including Together, Sakana, Modal and Physical Intelligence. I retain that attributed career record without inferring personal ownership, individual returns or a complete deal-leadership history.", outlook: "I am watching the mismatch between software-style growth expectations and the slower validation of physical systems. Compute infrastructure, robots and biology each face different capital and testing constraints. A promising portfolio theme needs evidence that customers can use the product at a viable cost, beyond the excitement of an early financing." },
  96: { hook: "Simon Willison's [September 12, 2022 prompt-injection post](https://simonwillison.net/2022/Sep/12/prompt-injection/) gives a dated practitioner account of attacks that mix untrusted content with instructions. The problem becomes more consequential when agents can act.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied degree award date against a primary university record.", career: "His [own biography](https://simonwillison.net/about/) identifies him as Datasette's creator and a Django co-creator and describes Lanyrd's acquisition by Eventbrite. It also documents his independent open-source work. I retain that first-person chronology and omit an unverified board-service history or precise current consulting schedule.", outlook: "I am watching whether practical demonstrations help teams set better boundaries around tools, data and permissions. His disclosed sponsorship and product-preview relationships are part of interpreting the writing, not evidence that it lacks value. A persuasive product impression is still different from an independent performance or security study." },
  97: { hook: "Nathan Lambert's [June 2, 2026 farewell to Ai2](https://www.interconnects.ai/p/farewell-ai2) is a direct account of open-model and post-training work. It also makes clear that a past Ai2 role should not be presented as a current appointment.", career: "The farewell describes work on Olmo models and reflects on the impact of open research despite performance limits. His [first-person career account](https://www.interconnects.ai/p/my-path-into-ai) documents the earlier doctoral and industry path. I omit precise internship dates, blanket priority claims for research terms and an unconfirmed current nonprofit title.", outlook: "I am watching whether open post-training releases expose enough data, code and evaluation detail for outside teams to reproduce the important choices. A label of open is weaker than a usable recipe. The next meaningful result would reduce the gap between reading a report and being able to train and assess a comparable model." },
  98: { hook: "Dwarkesh Patel's [podcast page](https://www.dwarkesh.com/about) describes deeply researched interviews. His long-form format lets AI researchers explain assumptions that a product announcement may leave implicit.", education: "I could not confirm a completed degree and award year from a primary university record.", career: "His own page identifies him as the host and links the podcast and other public work. The [May 2025 Sholto Douglas and Trenton Bricken interview](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/sholto-trenton-2) is one example of the format, covering reinforcement learning and interpretability. I omit an unverified birth date, audience count and donation chronology that are not needed to evaluate the contribution.", outlook: "I am watching whether interviews expose disagreements, concrete predictions and failure conditions that can be checked later. Access to a prominent guest is useful, but it is not independent verification of the guest's claims. A strong interview should leave a record that readers can compare against what actually happens." },
  99: { hook: "Shawn ‘swyx’ Wang's [AI Engineer organization history](https://www.ai.engineer/about) dates his AI-engineer essay and the first summit to 2023. It connects a practitioner role with a conference and learning community.", education: "I could not confirm the supplied formal education and bootcamp dates against original education records.", career: "The organization describes launching the summit with conference organizer Ben Dunphy and later expanding the events. [Latent Space's about page](https://www.latent.space/about) documents the newsletter and podcast. I do not repeat an unverified currency-trading asset figure or infer investment performance from a prior finance role.", outlook: "I am watching whether the community teaches repeatable engineering skills as quickly as it spreads fashionable claims. Useful evidence includes code, evaluation methods and candid failure reports that participants can reuse. Attendance and self-reported audience totals show reach, but do not measure whether a new practitioner can build a dependable system." },
  100: { hook: "Sebastian Raschka's [own biography](https://sebastianraschka.com/about/) describes reading papers and reference implementations, then building smaller versions in PyTorch. That code-first approach makes his explanations testable by learners.", career: "The [LLMs-from-scratch repository](https://github.com/rasbt/LLMs-from-scratch) accompanies his model-building work. [Manning's reasoning-model book page](https://www.manning.com/books/build-a-reasoning-model-from-scratch) supplies the publisher record for the later book. I leave out uncertain previous employment dates and a volatile star-count comparison with other frameworks; popularity is not a measure of whether a lesson reproduces a frontier system.", outlook: "I am watching whether the teaching makes assumptions, training choices and evaluation limits visible, particularly in reasoning and post-training. A small implementation can clarify an algorithm without reproducing the data, compute and engineering behind a commercial model. That distinction is essential when readers move from an educational example toward production work." },
  81: { hook: "Jonathan Godwin's [August 2026 Orbital announcement](https://www.orbitalindustries.com/news/press/basf-ecms-ai-materials-discovery-platform) describes licensing a materials-discovery platform to BASF Environmental Catalyst and Metal Solutions. That is a concrete industrial agreement to follow beyond a model benchmark.", career: "The statement identifies Godwin as Orbital Industries' CEO and describes CurieOS in catalyst-development work. The [Orb research paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.22570) provides a separate technical account of interatomic potentials. I do not infer his earlier employer from co-authorship with a lab's researchers, and leave out an unverified cooling-material sales story. A paper, an agreement and delivered industrial value are three different records.", outlook: "I am watching whether the industrial license produces useful experimental results and a repeatable business, rather than remaining a pilot. The [CurieOS announcement](https://www.orbitalindustries.com/news/announcements/introducing-curieos) explains the platform; customers' measured development time, costs and successful materials would be stronger evidence of its value than a general claim that AI accelerates discovery." },
  82: { hook: "Kristen DiCerbo's [Khan Academy writing](https://blog.khanacademy.org/student-motivation-hard-problem/) starts with the problem a school is trying to solve. That gives a useful test for AI tutoring: learning outcomes rather than the novelty of generated answers.", outlook: "I am watching whether AI assistance helps students explain and solve problems independently after the help is removed. Engagement and time in an app can be useful observations, but they do not establish improved understanding. Her learning-science background puts assessment, appropriate feedback and the design of a credible comparison at the center of that question." },
  83: { hook: "Alex Kendall's [own biography](https://alexgkendall.com/) dates Wayve's founding to 2017 and describes embodied AI for driving. The research question is whether learned systems generalize beyond the places and conditions they have seen.", career: "His publication record includes [work on uncertainty in computer vision](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.04977) and research on end-to-end driving. The biography identifies him as Wayve's co-founder and CEO after Cambridge research. I do not treat a planned robotaxi deployment as an already operating service, and omit uncertain financing totals and award dates from this entry.", outlook: "I am watching the conditions under which the system can operate, intervention rates and the cost of supervision. An integration agreement creates a path to distribution, not proof that a vehicle is ready for every road. The most useful evidence combines repeatable driving performance with a clear statement of operational limits." },
  84: { hook: "Beth Barnes's [METR organization page](https://metr.org/about) identifies her as founder and CEO. Its research asks how to measure increasingly capable agents without turning a narrow task suite into a universal forecast of automation.", career: "The organization describes independent evaluation and productivity research. I retain that institutional account rather than a precise early-employment chronology or updated doubling-time figure not established by the opened record. A time-horizon estimate depends on task selection, the success threshold and the evaluation setup; it is not a direct measure of how many jobs a model can replace.", outlook: "I am watching whether evaluation methods remain informative as tasks get longer and models become aware of testing. The useful result is a transparent comparison with uncertainty and reproducible conditions. A chart's extrapolated curve should not stand in for measured workplace productivity or the reliability needed for unsupervised deployment." },
  85: { hook: "Marius Hobbhahn's [Apollo team page](https://www.apolloresearch.ai/team) identifies him as CEO and co-founder of an organization studying AI scheming. Its work examines behavior under explicit experimental conditions.", career: "Apollo's own material connects research with a later monitoring product, Watcher. I omit an unverified conversion date, seed amount and exact anti-scheming reduction ratio rather than treat an intermediary compilation as an original experiment. Research on deceptive behavior needs its setup and limits alongside the headline: observing a behavior in a designed test does not establish its rate in every deployment.", outlook: "I am watching whether monitoring identifies useful warning signals in production without an unmanageable rate of false alarms. A model may behave differently when it recognizes an evaluation. The challenge is to show what the test establishes, what remains uncertain and whether safeguards still work when the model or environment changes." },
  86: { hook: "Buck Shlegeris's [Redwood team record](https://www.redwoodresearch.org/team) identifies him as CEO of an AI safety and security nonprofit. Its control research considers safeguards for systems whose cooperation cannot simply be assumed.", career: "The [AI control research material](https://redwoodresearch.org/research/ai-control) and his [April 2025 interview](https://80000hours.org/podcast/episodes/buck-shlegeris-ai-control-scheming/) describe monitoring and safeguards around potentially misaligned models. I leave the pre-2021 career chronology unknown and do not say one framework is the field's only method. The contribution is a way to test the deployment, not a certificate that every monitored model is safe.", outlook: "I am watching whether a safety case states the assumptions a control depends on and tests attempts to evade it. As agents receive more tools and permissions, failures of monitoring become economically consequential. A successful experiment with a cooperative model is weaker evidence than a test that challenges the safeguard's central assumptions." },
  87: { hook: "Owain Evans's [Truthful AI research page](https://truthful.ai/) lists work on emergent misalignment, subliminal learning and hidden influences on model answers. Narrow training choices can have consequences beyond the intended task.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied complete degree chronology against original university award records.", career: "Truthful AI identifies Evans as its team lead in Berkeley and lists the group's papers. Those artifacts let readers examine the conditions under which researchers observed broader behavior changes after particular training. I retain the experiment-level description rather than claim that every fine-tuned model becomes malicious, and leave out an unverified prior appointment timeline.", outlook: "I am watching whether the reported effects replicate across models, data choices and evaluation settings. Synthetic data is economically appealing, but hidden behavior transfer would make data provenance and testing more important. The conditions producing the result matter as much as the unsettling headline; the next test is knowing when the effect does and does not occur." },
  88: { hook: "[FAI's June 18, 2026 announcement](https://www.thefai.org/posts/dean-ball-joins-openai-as-head-of-strategic-futures) says Dean Ball joins OpenAI as head of strategic futures on July 6 after work on the White House AI Action Plan.", career: "The FAI record describes his plan co-authorship and continued nonresident fellowship. His [FAI biography](https://www.thefai.org/profile/dean-ball) documents the academic background. I omit uncertain dates for other appointments and do not infer a confirmed OpenAI IPO timetable. A role inside a lab gives his policy analysis a different institutional position from an independent newsletter.", outlook: "I am watching how frontier-model policy arguments change when their author works inside a major developer. Advocacy, scenario analysis and enacted rules are different forms of evidence. The useful test is whether proposals state their tradeoffs clearly and respond to contrary records, rather than presenting one company's strategic preferences as a neutral forecast." },
  89: { hook: "Lennart Heim's [training-compute paper](https://heim.xyz/documents/Training-Compute-Thresholds.pdf) examines when compute thresholds should trigger scrutiny of a model. The authors distinguish identifying a system for evaluation from deciding which mitigations it needs.", education: "I could not confirm degree awards and years against original university records.", career: "His [GovAI biography](https://governance.ai/team/lennart-heim) provides a record of compute-governance research. I do not infer a later Foundation role or control over a philanthropic budget from a social profile or roundup. The inspectable contribution is analysis of how compute can inform oversight without becoming a complete measure of model risk.", outlook: "I am watching whether resource-based thresholds remain useful as algorithmic efficiency and deployment methods change. A numerical boundary is easier to administer than a full capability assessment, but simplicity does not make it sufficient. The policy question is what additional testing and context are needed after a threshold is crossed." },
  90: { hook: "Kai Zenner's [OECD expert biography](https://oecd.ai/en/community/kai-zenner) identifies him as head of office and digital-policy adviser to Axel Voss MEP. That is a parliamentary role, not employment by the OECD.", career: "The expert biography describes his AI and digital-policy work and education, while his [industry-event biography](https://informaconnect.com/ai-regulation-forum/speakers/kai-zenner/) offers a later professional account. I leave out an unverified ranking, award and full prior-employment chronology. The role provides context for his policy commentary; it does not make every proposal he discusses settled law.", outlook: "I am watching how policy proposals become workable obligations for organizations deploying AI. Draft amendments, political positions and rules currently in force must remain separate. Useful analysis should state the stage of a proposal and the tradeoffs involved, rather than describe an expected change as if it has already taken effect." },
  71: { hook: "Deepak Pathak's [CMU research record](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~dpathak/) connects curiosity-driven learning with robots that learn from sensory data. His Skild AI work brings that generalization problem into a company.", outlook: "I am watching whether a shared model reliably controls different robot bodies and unfamiliar tasks. An announced partnership or financing does not establish that transfer. Useful evidence includes intervention rates, failure recovery and matched comparisons with specialized systems. I leave out an unverified round total and named customer-deployment claims from this entry." },
  72: { hook: "Bernt Børnich's 1X [announced NEO for homes in October 2025](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20251027434628/en/1X-Launches-NEO-The-Robot-Redefining-Life-at-Home). A home launch makes intervention, privacy and ordinary household conditions central to judging the product.", education: "I could not confirm his university degree or award year from a primary education record.", career: "The company release identifies Børnich and describes NEO as a home robot. I distinguish a launch and order offer from evidence that a robot can autonomously perform every advertised task in an unfamiliar home. The earlier company and product chronology is left out where I could not retrieve original dated records, rather than using a later promotional summary to fill the gaps.", outlook: "I am watching how much human intervention the system needs and what users understand about remote assistance. Factory capacity targets, preorders and shipped working units are different measures. The commercial test is useful, repeatable help at a support cost and privacy standard households can accept, not simply a compelling launch video." },
  73: { hook: "Carolina Parada co-authored [Google DeepMind's March 2025 Gemini Robotics announcement](https://deepmind.google/blog/gemini-robotics-brings-ai-into-the-physical-world/). It separates a vision-language-action model from a model focused on embodied reasoning.", education: "I have not confirmed the complete supplied degree chronology against primary university award records.", career: "The original company post describes bringing multimodal reasoning into physical actions and sets out the two model roles. Parada's [industry interview](https://www.automate.org/robotics/industry-insights/rip-it-up-and-start-again-google-deepmind-vp-on-rewriting-the-rules-of-robotics) gives a later account of the robotics work. I omit precise prior appointment dates and do not say one executive determines every partner's capabilities or that a announced model has already solved whole-body autonomy.", outlook: "I am watching whether reasoning produces reliable physical actions across different environments and robot hardware. A model that describes a plan persuasively still has to act safely under sensor uncertainty. Deployment evidence should include failures and recovery, not only demonstrations chosen because they worked." },
  74: { hook: "Max Junestrand's [Legora company record](https://legora.com/about) identifies him as co-founder and CEO of a legal-AI workspace. The buying question is whether drafting and review reduce the lawyer's complete workload.", education: "I could not confirm the supplied degree fields and award years against original university records.", career: "Legora's own material describes collaborative legal work, including review and drafting. That is a more direct product record than a claim that it is the only credible rival in a market. I omit a supplied interview count, a precise rebranding chronology and reported growth figures not corroborated by the readable company record in this review.", outlook: "I am watching whether repeat use and renewals show time saved after lawyers check the results. A generated draft can be fast while transferring substantial verification work to its user. Valuation talks are separate from closed financing, and neither alone demonstrates that an application fits the liability and confidentiality requirements of legal practice." },
  75: { hook: "Gabriel Pereyra's [March 2024 Harvey announcement](https://www.harvey.ai/blog/harvey-launches-ai-powered-professional-services-platform-on-microsoft-azure) connects model research with distribution through Microsoft Azure. It identifies him as co-founder and president.", career: "The company statement describes deployment of its professional-services platform on Azure. That is a dated commercial record, not proof that generated legal answers require no checking. I leave out an origin experiment's detailed success rate, uncertain research-employment dates and an implied personal stake that I could not establish from original records.", outlook: "I am watching whether specialized models and evaluations produce useful legal work at an acceptable review cost. Distribution can bring a product into more firms, but confidentiality, traceable sources and sustained renewals determine whether those firms keep using it. A reported customer count or funding round is different from evidence of correctness in a client's matter." },
  76: { hook: "George Sivulka's Hebbia turns document retrieval into an enterprise workflow. Its [June 2025 FlashDocs announcement](https://www.hebbia.com/blog/acquiring-flashdocs-streamlining-financial-workflows-with-ai-slide-deck) adds slide creation after research and analysis.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied degree award and withdrawal dates against original university records.", career: "The company announcement identifies Sivulka as Hebbia's founder and CEO and describes buying FlashDocs to create branded slides from research. The announcement establishes the product move, not undisclosed acquisition consideration. I omit an unverified early banker-prototype story and exact internship years while retaining the direct connection between document analysis and presentation.", outlook: "I am watching whether source citations survive every stage from retrieval to a final deck. More polished output can make an unsupported conclusion harder to spot. The useful commercial test is less work after checking and correction, not just a faster first draft or an attractive presentation." },
  77: { hook: "Gabe Stengel's [April 29, 2026 Rogo announcement](https://rogo.com/blog/series-d) records $160 million in new financing led by Kleiner Perkins. It describes expansion of AI tools for financial professionals.", career: "The [January 2026 company post](https://rogo.com/blog/scaling-rogo-to-build-the-future-of-investment-banking-our-75m-series-c-and-european-expansion) records an earlier $75 million Series C and European expansion. The April announcement identifies returning participants including Sequoia, Thrive and J.P. Morgan Growth Equity Partners. I keep the two dated rounds separate and do not infer an undisclosed valuation or a complete career chronology from the financing releases.", outlook: "I am watching whether analyst agents improve verified work rather than just accelerate output. Financial research needs source provenance, consistent calculations and review of assumptions. Forward-deployed delivery can help fit a workflow, but its labor cost must be distinguished from scalable software economics." },
  78: { hook: "[Helsing's January 2026 announcement](https://helsing.ai/newsroom/chief-scientist-antoine-bordes-receives-aaai-classic-paper-award) identifies Antoine Bordes as chief scientist and recognizes co-authored research on structured knowledge embeddings.", education: "I could not confirm the supplied doctorate institution and award year from an original education record.", career: "The company account connects his earlier research to an AAAI Classic Paper Award. I retain the team attribution and separate the scientific contribution from a claim that it proves operational defense performance. An award can document the influence of an earlier method without certifying the reliability of every later system using AI.", outlook: "I am watching how autonomy is bounded and tested in operational systems. Perception, structured representations and uncertainty all matter when a model's outputs have physical consequences. I omit supplied funding and aircraft-delivery claims not needed to establish his research role; a future flight target is not a completed demonstration." },
  79: { hook: "Oege de Moor's XBOW [described reaching the top of HackerOne's US leaderboard in June 2025](https://xbow.com/blog/top-1-how-xbow-did-it). The company's own account includes human review before reports were submitted.", career: "[GitHub's September 2019 announcement](https://github.blog/news-insights/company-news/github-welcomes-semmle/) records its acquisition of Semmle, the earlier business associated with de Moor. XBOW's later account describes automated discovery supported by a security team reviewing submissions under platform rules. I do not present that workflow as fully independent of human labor or turn a leaderboard position into proof that every report was novel and accepted.", outlook: "I am watching accepted, actionable findings and time to remediation rather than raw discovery counts. A fast automated test has commercial value when it reliably finds problems a customer can fix without flooding the team with duplicates and false positives. Scope, authorization and hidden review labor affect that value." },
  80: { hook: "GNoME's [Google DeepMind account](https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/millions-of-new-materials-discovered-with-deep-learning/) describes 380,000 predicted stable crystals. Ekin Dogus Cubuk's later Periodic Labs work moves the question from computational predictions to physical experiments.", career: "The GNoME account describes a large predicted-materials search, while [Periodic's September 30, 2025 founding statement](https://periodic.com/news/introducing-periodic-labs) lays out autonomous laboratories feeding experimental results back into models. I keep a predicted stable structure separate from a material synthesized, characterized and found useful. I omit a volatile citation count and unverified deployment details from the supplied biography.", outlook: "[Andreessen Horowitz's founding announcement](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/) records $300 million of funding without a disclosed valuation. I am watching whether the closed experimental loop produces independently reproducible materials results at a useful cost. Capital buys a research attempt; it does not establish a commercially valuable discovery." },
  61: { hook: "[March 2026 reporting](https://tech.eu/2026/03/25/granola-raises-125m-at-1-5bn-valuation/) records Granola's $125 million Series C at a $1.5 billion valuation. Chris Pedregal's app is moving from meeting notes toward shared business context.", career: "Granola's [release archive](https://www.granola.ai/updates) names Pedregal as an author of product updates and also names co-founder Sam Stephenson. It documents meeting chat, note sharing and team-context features. I leave out unconfirmed previous acquisition dates, a precise intervening Google employment timeline and a cumulative funding sum; the release record is the direct evidence of what this product is adding.", outlook: "I am watching whether shared context helps teams retrieve decisions without losing nuance or exposing sensitive conversations. A meeting summary is useful only when its users understand what was recorded, what was inferred and who can access it. Enterprise expansion adds permissions and retention requirements alongside the convenience that initially draws individual users." },
  62: { hook: "Zachary Lipton's [Abridge author biography](https://www.abridge.com/author/zack-lipton) connects academic research on reliable machine learning with scientific and technology leadership at an AI documentation company.", career: "His [CMU expert record](https://www.cmu.edu/news/experts/zachary.lipton) documents his academic work, while Abridge's biography identifies the company role. Those records connect research on robustness with a product used in clinical workflows. I omit uncertain appointment years, award rankings and later investment terms that I could not establish from original records during this review.", outlook: "I am watching whether less documentation effort comes with evidence that omissions, transcription errors and misleading summaries are detected and corrected. Clinical and billing workflows give those mistakes consequences beyond a weak benchmark score. An investment or product expansion is not, by itself, proof of improved patient outcomes." },
  63: { hook: "[Ambience's September 22, 2025 announcement](https://www.ambiencehealthcare.com/blog/ambience-healthcare-announces-nikhil-buduma-as-new-chief-executive-officer) appoints Nikhil Buduma CEO after serving as co-founder and chief scientist. It places documentation, coding and clinical workflows in the same product strategy.", education: "I have not verified the supplied degree dates, school honors and Olympiad medals against original award records.", career: "The original appointment statement says co-founder Mike Ng becomes president and chairman. It gives a definite September 2025 date, stronger than the promotional biography's undated leadership claim. I use this company record for the transition and omit a supplied early-career chronology that could not be corroborated. Scientific leadership and executive responsibility are separate roles, even when held sequentially by the same founder.", outlook: "I am watching whether documentation and coding tools improve the complete workflow without introducing errors that clinicians or billing teams must repair. Revenue integrity is commercially attractive, but it needs reliable evidence about accuracy and deployment. I leave out award-based conclusions about clinical effectiveness and unverified private valuation totals." },
  64: { hook: "Zachary Ziegler connects language-model research with OpenEvidence's clinician information product. [Sequoia's founder record](https://sequoiacap.com/founder/zachary-ziegler/) provides a direct association with the company, while his earlier paper can be inspected separately.", education: "I have not confirmed a completed Harvard doctorate or undergraduate award record, so I do not infer either from research affiliation.", career: "[PMLR's ICML 2019 record](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/ziegler19a.html) identifies Ziegler and Alexander Rush as authors of Latent Normalizing Flows for Discrete Sequences. The later [Sequoia page](https://sequoiacap.com/founder/zachary-ziegler/) associates him with OpenEvidence. I leave out unverified counts of physicians and consultations, and do not treat an investor biography as proof of clinical safety.", outlook: "I am watching whether cited medical answers accurately reflect the underlying evidence and help a clinician find what is relevant. A citation can look authoritative without supporting the generated sentence. Usage and private valuations are business signals, but evaluation of answer quality and appropriate escalation are different tests." },
  65: { hook: "Suchi Saria's clinical AI work has a published outcomes record, which makes study design central to evaluating it. A deployed prediction system still needs evidence about what clinicians did after seeing an alert.", career: "Her [Johns Hopkins biography](https://publichealth.jhu.edu/faculty/2783/suchi-saria) documents the academic background, and [Bayesian Health's company history](https://www.bayesianhealth.com/about) identifies her as founder. TREWS outcomes have been studied in clinical deployment. I avoid converting an observational association into a promise that every deployment reduces mortality, and omit earlier employment and award details not established by the original records used here.", outlook: "I am watching whether clinical decision support improves timely action in settings beyond the original study. The important evidence includes who was compared, whether clinicians responded and how confounding was addressed. Prediction accuracy, clinician satisfaction and patient outcomes are different measures and should not be collapsed into one claim of effectiveness." },
  66: { hook: "Joshua Meier's [Index Ventures founder account](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/transforming-biology-from-science-to-engineering-our-investment-in-chai-discovery/) connects work at OpenAI, Meta FAIR and Absci with co-founding Chai Discovery, a company using AI for molecular design.", education: "The investor account says he graduated from Harvard with computer science and chemistry majors. It does not establish the award year, so I leave that date out.", career: "Index names Meier, Jacques Boitreaud, Matthew McPartlon and Jack Dent as Chai's founders and dates Meier's OpenAI move to 2018. It describes a subsequent move through Meta FAIR and Absci toward AI applied to biology. The research preprint provides a separate record of design experiments; a successful laboratory binder is not proof of a safe or effective therapeutic.", outlook: "I am watching whether designed molecules advance through reproducible laboratory validation and the later development steps needed for medicines. I omit unverified partnership and later financing figures from this entry. A licensing announcement or funding round would not establish that clinical testing has succeeded." },
  67: { hook: "Brian Hie's Evo 2 work applies long-context modeling to genomic data. [Arc Institute's release](https://arcinstitute.org/news/evo2) identifies him as a co-senior author and describes releasing code, data and weights for outside research.", outlook: "I am watching what external researchers can reproduce from the released artifacts and how limits on biological design are evaluated. His Stanford and Arc roles connect research with release decisions. A genome model's prediction performance does not prove that a designed organism will behave as intended or that a model is ready for clinical diagnosis." },
  68: { career: "[Isomorphic's November 26, 2025 announcement](https://www.isomorphiclabs.com/articles/isomorphic-labs-to-appoint-max-jaderberg-as-president) appoints Jaderberg president effective January 1, 2026, after serving as chief AI officer. It describes him as a founding team member and credits his work building the company's AI approach. His [Broad Institute biography](https://www.broadinstitute.org/bios/max-jaderberg) documents earlier Oxford and machine-learning work. I omit a fuller acquisition and project-credit chronology not needed to establish that transition." },
  69: { hook: "Emma Lundberg's [Stanford biography](https://profiles.stanford.edu/emma-lundberg?tab=bio) identifies her as director of the Human Protein Atlas Cell Atlas and connects imaging, proteomics and AI with efforts to model cells.", career: "The institutional biography describes Stanford bioengineering and pathology appointments alongside a KTH professorship. Her [2024 Stanford research account](https://news.stanford.edu/stories/2024/12/scientists-call-for-all-out-global-effort-to-create-an-ai-virtual-cell) presents the case for a global virtual-cell effort. I retain that research connection without an unverified chronology of advisory boards, administrative posts or specific later preprint output counts.", outlook: "I am watching whether integrated cell models generate predictions that survive experiments outside their training data. Imaging quality, annotation and the completeness of measured cell states all affect the result. A convincing simulated cell is a research artifact; the business and scientific value depend on what it helps discover and validate." },
  70: { hook: "Karol Hausman's [own biography](https://karolhausman.github.io/) identifies him as a Physical Intelligence co-founder after robot-learning work at Google DeepMind. His question is how a robot acquires general-purpose skills in the real world.", education: "I have not confirmed all supplied degree award dates against original university records.", career: "The biography records an earlier Google DeepMind staff research-scientist role and Stanford adjunct teaching, including deep reinforcement learning. It also describes RT-X as a collaboration across many authors and institutions. That is a useful reminder that robot-learning systems depend on shared datasets and infrastructure, rather than the contribution of one founder alone.", outlook: "I am watching whether a general-purpose robot policy transfers across bodies, tasks and unfamiliar environments without substantial custom engineering. Demonstrations need to expose failures, setup labor and the conditions under which they work. I leave later private financing talks out of this entry rather than infer that a reported round closed." },
  51: { hook: "n8n's [press record](https://n8n.io/press/) dates Jan Oberhauser's founding of the company to 2019 and records a May 2026 SAP strategic investment at a $5.2 billion valuation. That is a company transaction measure, not his personal wealth.", career: "The company identifies Oberhauser as founder and CEO and describes its fair-code licensing under the Sustainable Use License. Its financing chronology lists a $1.5 million seed in March 2020, $12 million Series A in April 2021, $60 million Series B in March 2025 and $180 million Series C at $2.5 billion in October 2025. I use those company figures rather than mixing currency conversions from different reports.", outlook: "I am watching whether workflow automation becomes dependable infrastructure for corporate agents. The SAP relationship adds an enterprise distribution channel, but does not guarantee adoption. Licensing, debugging and governance remain part of the buying decision: a workflow that works in a demo still needs clear ownership when a connected system changes or an automated action fails." },
  52: { hook: "Robin Rombach's [Stanford seminar biography](https://ee.stanford.edu/event/10-16-2024/latent-diffusion-flux-and-beyond-scaling-efficient-content-creation) connects latent diffusion and Stable Diffusion research to co-founding Black Forest Labs.", career: "The October 2024 biography describes earlier research directorship at Stability AI and work on VQGAN, Latent Diffusion and Stable Diffusion. It identifies efficient visual generation and open models as the thread connecting that work. [December 2025 reporting](https://techcrunch.com/2025/12/01/black-forest-labs-raises-300m-at-3-25b-valuation) describes a $300 million Series B at a $3.25 billion valuation. I attribute that financing to the report and omit an unverified list of model customers.", outlook: "I am watching whether a specialist model lab retains paying customers as large creative platforms develop or bundle competing tools. Image quality needs to be judged alongside serving cost, retries, controllability and rights requirements. A financing price shows investor expectations; reliable production use determines how much of those expectations can become revenue." },
  53: { hook: "[SaaStr's May 2026 speaker materials](https://saastrannual.com/speaker/alex-mashrabov) describe Higgsfield at a $300 million revenue run rate and identify Alex Mashrabov as CEO and co-founder. I treat that conference biography as an attributed company account, not audited ARR.", education: "I could not confirm the supplied degree details from an original university record.", career: "The same conference biography describes earlier generative-AI leadership at Snap and co-founding AI Factory, acquired by Snap for a reported $166 million. That is acquisition consideration, not his individual proceeds. Higgsfield's later business applies video models in a consumer creation workflow. I omit an aggregator's month-by-month revenue progression and uncertain education history.", outlook: "I am watching whether a workflow business built around rapidly improving models can preserve repeat demand and margins. If users only arrive for a viral effect, recurring revenue will be harder to sustain. The useful tests are paid retention, cost per completed creation and the number of retries needed for an acceptable output." },
  54: { hook: "[Gamma's November 2025 company account](https://gamma.app/insights/how-we-built-a-usd100m-business-differently) reports $100 million ARR and profitability with about 50 employees. Grant Lee's business offers a concrete case for examining a small team's operating economics." },
  55: { hook: "[Profound's September 15, 2026 announcement](https://www.tryprofound.com/newsroom/profound-raises-usd180m-series-d-at-usd1-8b-valuation-to-build-the-ai-platform-for-marketing-teams) records a $180 million Series D at a $1.8 billion valuation. James Cadwallader is building a business around how brands appear in AI-generated answers.", education: "I could not confirm a formal education history from a primary record, so I omit the supplied university-attendance claim.", career: "The announcement identifies Cadwallader as co-founder and CEO and describes a progression from AI-discovery analytics to marketing agents and orchestration. It reports more than 1,000 enterprise brands and names customers, but those are company claims. I leave out an unverified early sales story and precise previous employment dates that do not establish the product's performance.", outlook: "I am watching whether visibility in an AI answer leads to discovery, purchases or other measurable customer value. A mention count can rise without a sale, and measurement is difficult when answers vary by user and prompt. Profound needs to show the connection between its analytics, the actions marketers take and the resulting business outcome." },
  56: { hook: "Noah Shinn is the first listed author of [Reflexion](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366), a framework in which agents use verbal feedback rather than updating model weights. His later consumer-agent company, Instinct, puts feedback and error recovery into consequential everyday tasks.", education: "I could not confirm the supplied university-withdrawal date or age from an original education record.", career: "The Reflexion paper, submitted in March 2023, names Shinn alongside five other authors, including Shunyu Yao. Its contribution is a method for storing feedback as text to improve later attempts. [September 2026 reporting](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/28/viral-ai-agent-instinct-raises-1b-series-c-at-a-10b-valuation/) connects Shinn to Instinct. I omit uncertain founding and launch dates rather than choosing between conflicting intermediary profiles.", outlook: "The September 28 report describes a $1 billion Series C at a $10 billion valuation. I am watching whether a consumer agent can earn repeat paid use while giving users clear permissions, recoverable actions and a reliable account of mistakes. A large financing does not establish sustainable revenue or safe delegation." },
  57: { hook: "Lourdes Agapito's [UCL impact case study](https://www.ucl.ac.uk/research-innovation/case-studies/2022/apr/ucl-computer-scientists-create-photorealistic-talking-digital-actors) connects visual-synthesis research with co-founding Synthesia and its digital actors.", education: "I have not confirmed every supplied degree and appointment date against original university records, so I omit the full dated chronology.", career: "The university case study attributes underpinning research to Agapito's team and describes commercial use of photorealistic talking digital actors. That documents a path from academic computer vision to a production tool, without making every later improvement the work of one founder. [January 2026 financing reporting](https://www.finsmes.com/2026/01/synthesia-raises-200m-in-series-e-funding-at-4-billion-post-money-valuation-2.html) describes a $200 million Series E at a $4 billion post-money valuation.", outlook: "I am watching whether enterprise video creation remains useful after the novelty of realistic avatars fades. Production economics include editing time, localization and appropriate identity controls. A lower creation cost has commercial value only when the resulting communication is accurate, acceptable to its audience and easy to update." },
  58: { hook: "Demi Guo co-founded Pika, whose [company team page](https://pika.art/careers) connects her mathematics and computer-science background with building video-creation tools.", education: "The company biography describes mathematics and computer-science study at Harvard and AI research at Stanford. It does not establish all degree award dates, so I do not infer them.", career: "Pika's own page identifies Guo as CEO and co-founder, names Chenlin Meng in the team and describes earlier work at Meta, Google, Microsoft and Epic Games. I use that attributed company biography and leave out exact internship dates. [Her November 2023 post](https://x.com/demi_guo_/status/1729546758718656530) provides a dated public launch record rather than a complete capitalization history.", outlook: "I am watching whether video generation and personal avatars lead to repeat creative use at sustainable serving costs. Consumer attention can move quickly when rival models improve. Production control, editing and the ability to deliver an acceptable result without excessive retries are stronger tests than a conflicting private valuation estimate." },
  59: { hook: "Mikey Shulman's [Suno company posts](https://www.suno.com/blog) describe a business moving from music generation toward releases and partnerships with the music industry. The rights relationships are part of the product's economics, not an issue separate from growth.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied doctorate award year against an original university record.", career: "Suno's September 2026 blog identifies Shulman as co-founder and CEO and dates an announcement of new music models in partnership with the industry. [Music Business Worldwide's October account](https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/suno-is-far-beyond-2m-subscribers-and-300m-in-revenue-says-mikey-shulman-who-argues-its-good-that-ai-music-has-no-firm-rules-of-the-road-yet/) attributes subscriber and revenue claims to him. I do not substitute those assertions for audited results, or repeat a later lawsuit's exact recording count without the court record.", outlook: "I am watching whether licensing relationships, creator acceptance and recurring demand support a durable business. Faster generation is only one input: model-serving expense and rights costs both affect margins. I leave legal outcomes open rather than predict that one case will settle copyright rules for the whole industry." },
  60: { hook: "Wonderful's [own company page](https://www.wonderful.ai/about-us) promotes a $550 million Series C and identifies Bar Winkler among its founders. Its enterprise-agent strategy relies on local deployment teams as well as software.", career: "[TechCrunch's March 2026 report](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/12/wonderful-raises-150m-series-b-at-2b-valuation/) describes an earlier $150 million Series B at a $2 billion valuation. The company describes agents and embedded delivery for enterprise workflows. I do not infer that services work is negligible, and leave out customer counts, staffing totals and earlier acquisition dates not established by the records reviewed here.", outlook: "I am watching whether localized deployment can scale without equally fast growth in implementation labor. That approach may open markets underserved by English-first products, but recurring software revenue and one-off delivery work have different economics. The financing headline does not by itself establish margins, customer retention or founder wealth." },
  41: { hook: "Jerry Liu's LlamaIndex connects open-source retrieval tools with a managed document-processing business. [KPMG's May 2025 investment statement](https://kpmg.com/us/en/media/news/llamaIndex-announces-investments-from-databricks-and-kpmg.html) identifies him as co-founder and CEO.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied degree, honors and award chronology against a primary university record.", career: "The [KPMG announcement](https://kpmg.com/us/en/media/news/llamaIndex-announces-investments-from-databricks-and-kpmg.html) describes managed parsing, ingestion and retrieval and investments from KPMG and Databricks. The [open repository](https://github.com/run-llama/llama_index) provides the technical project record. I leave out exact earlier employment years from an aggregator and an uncorroborated total-funding calculation: investments disclosed in separate announcements do not automatically sum to a complete capitalization history.", outlook: "[TechCrunch's March 2025 report](https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/04/llamaindex-launches-a-cloud-service-for-building-unstructed-data-agents) describes a $19 million Series A led by Norwest. I am watching whether better document parsing and retrieval improve agent reliability on real enterprise data. An impressive final answer can still depend on a flawed extraction step upstream." },
  42: { hook: "Anton Osika's Lovable announced a [$400 million Series C at a $13.3 billion valuation in August 2026](https://lovable.dev/blog/series-c). That financing gives a concrete scale to the question: can software generated from natural-language instructions become dependable business infrastructure?", career: "[KTH's 2025 award profile](https://www.kth.se/en/om/nyheter/centrala-nyheter/anton-osika-och-fabian-hedin-kth-innovation-award-2025-1.1425893) identifies Osika and Fabian Hedin as Lovable's founders. The [August 12, 2026 company announcement](https://lovable.dev/blog/series-c) dates the product launch to November 2024 and reports more than 60 million projects created. That is a company usage claim, not a count of successful or maintained businesses.", outlook: "The round was led by Menlo Ventures and co-led by the Scaleup Europe Fund managed by EQT. I am watching whether rapid app creation translates into durable paid use, secure deployments and manageable maintenance. A forecast revenue run rate is different from realized annual revenue, and a project count says little about repair costs after launch." },
  43: { hook: "[Wix's June 18, 2025 acquisition announcement](https://www.wix.com/press-room/home/post/wix-further-expands-into-vibe-coding-with-acquisition-of-base44-a-hyper-growth-startup-that-simplif) records approximately $80 million of initial consideration for Maor Shlomo's Base44, including employee retention payments, plus conditional earn-outs through 2029.", career: "The release identifies Shlomo as Base44's CEO and says the product will operate as a distinct business within Wix. About $25 million in employee retention bonuses was expected within the initial consideration, rather than as an additional founder payout. I do not translate the headline acquisition price into Shlomo's wealth, and omit unverified claims about his earlier company title, personal profit and individual share of the deal.", outlook: "I am watching whether a conversational app builder keeps its speed and customer appeal inside a public company. The conditional earn-outs make future performance part of the transaction, but do not establish that all payments have been earned. I examine the ownership and deal terms separately in [Who Owns Base44?](https://visionarytalks.com/who-owns-base44/)." },
  44: { hook: "[ElevenLabs' May 2026 update](https://elevenlabs.io/blog/500m-arr-and-new-investors) reports that ARR passed $500 million in the first four months of the year, after ending 2025 at $350 million. Mati Staniszewski co-founded the business turning voice models into enterprise products.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied school and degree award dates against primary education records.", career: "The [company's own history](https://elevenlabs.io/about) identifies Staniszewski and Piotr Dabkowski as founders. Its May operating update describes enterprises using voice agents and a further close in the Series D financing. I use that direct business record and leave out uncertain earlier job dates, board appointments and a team-acquisition chronology that is not needed to explain the product.", outlook: "I am watching whether enterprise voice agents produce reliable service outcomes with workable margins and appropriate control over synthetic voices. ARR is a company-reported recurring-revenue measure, not audited annual revenue or cash generation. A possible IPO date is a reported intention, not a confirmed listing schedule." },
  45: { hook: "Brendan Foody's Mercor organizes domain experts to help train and evaluate AI systems. The [company mission](https://www.mercor.com/mission/) frames human expertise as a bottleneck, which makes the distinction between contractor billings and retained revenue essential.", education: "[KTVU's account](https://www.ktvu.com/news/from-bay-area-high-school-friends-worlds-youngest-self-made-billionaires-company-valued-10b) documents the founders' shared high-school background. I omit unconfirmed precise college-withdrawal and award dates.", career: "The [company's mission page](https://www.mercor.com/mission/) names Foody and Adarsh Hiremath in its leadership account and describes the expert network and APEX evaluation work. [TechCrunch's April 2026 report](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/09/after-data-breach-10b-valued-startup-mercor-is-having-a-month/) dates a $350 million round at a $10 billion valuation to October 2025. That is a company financing price, not an independently verified personal net worth.", outlook: "[TechCrunch's July 9, 2026 report](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/09/mercor-is-in-talks-for-a-20b-valuation/) describes $20 billion valuation talks, not a closed round. I am watching whether client trust and contractor economics hold up as expert-data demand grows, while keeping gross billings separate from money the company retains." },
  46: { hook: "[Decagon's January 27, 2026 announcement](https://decagon.ai/blog/series-d-announcement) records $250 million of new funding at a $4.5 billion valuation. Jesse Zhang co-founded a business whose agents have to resolve customer problems, not merely handle more messages.", education: "I have not verified a degree award year from an original university record, so I leave out the supplied age and precise education chronology.", career: "The [company page](https://decagon.ai/about) identifies Zhang as co-founder and CEO and Ashwin Sreenivas as co-founder and president. That corrects the supplied CTO label. The financing announcement describes expansion of the AI concierge business with Coatue and Index leading the new round. I omit unsupported acquisition details and customer lists rather than treating a blocked Forbes page as a verified record.", outlook: "I am watching resolution quality, repeat contacts and the ease of reaching a person when automation fails. A high private valuation does not establish revenue or profitability; the paid product must earn renewals through customer outcomes. Reducing escalation by frustrating users would make a containment statistic look better while damaging the business." },
  47: { hook: "[Factory's September 15, 2026 announcement](https://factory.com/news/5-billion-valuation) records a $200 million financing at a $5 billion valuation. The original date is September 15, correcting the supplied September 16 chronology.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied degree and doctoral-withdrawal dates against primary university records.", career: "The statement is signed by Matan Grinberg and Eno Reyes, and dates Factory's founding to 2023. It names Blackstone, Khosla Ventures and Sequoia among the financing participants and says total funding exceeds $400 million. I use that original record instead of summing an aggregator's intermediate rounds or repeating an unverified cold-email origin story.", outlook: "I am watching whether enterprise coding agents reduce the complete cost of software delivery: implementation, review, security and ongoing maintenance. The company's announcement describes controlled deployment across cloud, on-premises and air-gapped environments. Those are product claims that customer results need to substantiate, rather than proof that greater code output automatically creates value." },
  48: { hook: "[TechCrunch's February 17, 2026 report](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/17/emergent-hits-100m-arr-eight-months-after-launch-rolls-out-mobile-app/) attributes an annual revenue run rate above $100 million to Emergent, eight months after launch. That is a company claim rather than audited annual sales.", career: "The [company's April 2026 Wingman announcement](https://emergent.sh/news/emergent-launches-wingman-autonomous-ai-agent) identifies Mukund Jha as co-founder and CEO and describes agents operating through connected business tools. [January 2026 reporting](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/20/indian-vibe-coding-startup-emergent-raises-70m-at-300m-valuation-from-softbank-khosla-ventures/) records $70 million raised at a $300 million valuation. I omit an unverified later round and his brother's detailed education history.", outlook: "I am watching whether nontechnical users retain control when delegated software acts across calendars, messaging and other business systems. Creation speed and reported adoption are useful leads, but renewals, error recovery and permissions are stronger evidence that an agent is ready for consequential work." },
  49: { hook: "[February 2026 reporting](https://techfundingnews.com/runway-315m-series-e-5-3b-valuation-world-models/) records a $315 million Runway Series E at a $5.3 billion valuation. Cristóbal Valenzuela's company is directing investment toward world models as well as creative video tools.", education: "I have not verified the supplied complete degree chronology against original university records, so I omit exact award years.", career: "Runway's [own summit materials](https://summit.runway.com/ny) identify Valenzuela and Anastasis Germanidis as co-founders and co-CEOs. The February funding report names General Atlantic as lead investor and describes the company's expansion from media tools toward simulation. I leave out unverified teaching dates and earlier deal totals; a later financing headline does not certify every stage of a founder's biography.", outlook: "I am watching whether world models support useful applications beyond selected creative samples. Work in robotics, medicine or climate requires different evaluation standards from an attractive video. Serving costs, manual correction and dependable behavior will decide how much of that broader ambition becomes a business." },
  50: { hook: "Clay's [September 9, 2026 company announcement](https://www.clay.com/blog/series-d) records $115 million in Series D funding at a $7.1 billion valuation. Kareem Amin's business sits at the intersection of data enrichment and AI-assisted sales workflows.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied degree award year from an original university record.", career: "The [January 2026 tender announcement](https://www.clay.com/blog/tender-offer-2026) describes employee share liquidity, while the September statement describes new financing. These are separate transactions: a tender transfers existing shares and does not automatically add the same amount to company cash. I omit the supplied early-career dates and uncorroborated total funding history.", outlook: "The September statement reports fourfold revenue growth in 2025 and more than 17,000 customers. I treat those as company operating claims, not audited accounts. I am watching whether data enrichment and automated outreach improve business outcomes without degrading data quality or merely generating more low-value activity." },
  32: { hook: "Gorkem Yurtseven co-founded fal, an infrastructure platform for generative image, video and audio models. Its [company history](https://fal.ai/about) dates the founding with Burkay Gur to 2021.", career: "The company connects its origin to the founders' earlier work at Amazon and Coinbase. [TechCrunch's December 2025 funding report](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/fal-nabs-140m-fresh-funding-222133234.html) describes Yurtseven as an ex-Amazon developer and reports fal's expansion in media-model hosting. I use co-founder as his title because the accessible profiles disagree about the executive title. I omit unsourced customer lists and treat revenue reporting as company context rather than audited accounts.", outlook: "The December report records $140 million in new funding at a $4.5 billion valuation, led by Sequoia. It also attributes revenue above $200 million as of October to Bloomberg reporting, without establishing a comparable annual recurring-revenue measure. I am watching whether a specialist serving layer retains customers as model providers change their prices and distribution." },
  33: { hook: "Jeffrey Morgan co-founded Ollama with Michael Chiang, according to [Y Combinator's company record](https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/ollama). The tool makes running open models locally easier, turning installation and distribution into part of the AI business story.", career: "[TechCrunch's March 2015 account](https://techcrunch.com/2015/03/12/kitematic-founders-barely-two-years-out-of-college-sell-to-docker) describes the earlier Kitematic acquisition by Docker. YC's later Ollama record identifies Morgan and Chiang as founders. The progression connects two developer tools that reduce setup complexity, but the acquisition story does not establish the price, Morgan's proceeds or a full intervening employment chronology.", outlook: "[Pulse 2.0's July 2026 report](https://pulse2.com/ollama-raises-65-million-in-series-b-funding-to-expand-its-open-source-ai-platform/) describes a $65 million Series B led by Theory Ventures and attributes an 8.9 million-developer figure to the company. I am watching whether that distribution supports a sustainable local and hybrid-inference business. A user count does not by itself establish paid customers, revenue or margins." },
  34: { hook: "Simon Hørup Eskildsen's [own career record](https://sirupsen.com/about/) dates turbopuffer to 2023 after eight years working on Shopify infrastructure. Its object-storage approach makes retrieval cost the central question.", career: "His biography dates Shopify work to 2013 to 2021, consulting with companies including Replicate and Readwise to 2021 to 2023, and turbopuffer's founding to 2023. He describes infrastructure scaling from 1,000 to one million requests per second and maintains the napkin-math project. Those are attributed first-person career records. I leave out a particular job-offer story and exact customer savings that I could not corroborate with a readable original account.", outlook: "I am watching whether low-cost storage also produces dependable retrieval latency and fresh context at scale. A cheaper backend matters to an AI product only if it can return the right material within the product's response budget. Capacity, paid usage and customer outcomes are more useful here than an unsourced growth multiple." },
  35: { hook: "Ankur Goyal's [Braintrust funding announcement](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-b) connects his earlier work at Impira and Figma to a company selling evaluation and observability tools for production AI.", education: "I could not confirm the supplied degree or withdrawal chronology from a primary education record.", career: "In the February 2026 post, Goyal says he built internal evaluation tools at both Impira and Figma and founded Braintrust after seeing the same need elsewhere. That first-person explanation establishes the product's origin without requiring an aggregator's precise promotion or employment dates. The company describes a shift from experiments to agents embedded in engineering workflows, where tracing failures becomes an operational requirement.", outlook: "The [February 17, 2026 announcement](https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/announcing-series-b) confirms an $80 million Series B led by ICONIQ. It does not establish the post-money valuation in the readable text I reviewed, so I omit that figure. I am watching whether evaluations detect failures customers actually encounter and help teams improve them, rather than merely add another dashboard." },
  36: { hook: "Anastasios Angelopoulos's [own biography](https://angelopoulos.ai/) identifies him as Arena's co-founder and CEO and describes a research focus on reliable AI. That puts a researcher in charge of a business whose central asset is trust in evaluation.", career: "His biography documents a Berkeley postdoc after doctoral work, and publications on conformal prediction and prediction-powered inference. The later Arena role connects those reliability questions to public model comparisons. I avoid treating a leaderboard position as a universal verdict: rankings depend on the tasks, users and comparison method. The record also does not make one founder solely responsible for the platform's research or every ranked outcome.", outlook: "I am watching whether Arena can run commercial evaluation services while maintaining confidence in the independence of its comparisons. [January 2026 reporting](https://bridgemena.com/news/lmarena-reaches-1-7b-valuation-following-150m-series-a-round) describes a $150 million Series A at a $1.7 billion post-money valuation. I attribute that price to the funding report and leave out unverified operating run rates." },
  37: { hook: "David Soria Parra is a lead maintainer in the [Model Context Protocol's October 2026 maintainer record](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/main/MAINTAINERS.md). The protocol makes tool and data connections a shared infrastructure problem.", education: "I could not confirm his university or degree from an original education record.", career: "The [AI Engineer biography](https://www.ai.engineer/speakers/david-soria-parra) connects his PHP and developer-tooling background to MCP. The protocol's own maintainer list names both Soria Parra and Den Delimarsky as lead maintainers and identifies Justin Spahr-Summers as co-inventor and emeritus lead maintainer. I retain the team attribution and omit an unverified full employment chronology.", outlook: "I am watching whether the ecosystem converges on compatible authentication and authorization behavior. Support in a client is not enough if a connection grants excessive permissions or behaves differently across implementations. The economic benefit is easier integration with less bespoke maintenance; that benefit needs interoperable releases rather than a repository popularity count." },
  39: { hook: "Reiner Pope's [MatX company biography](https://matx.com/jobs) identifies him as co-founder and CEO and describes earlier work as Google's PaLM efficiency lead. His startup turns inference efficiency into a hardware business.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied degree year from an original university record.", career: "The MatX biography attributes high-performance software and hardware experience to Pope, including PaLM inference work and involvement in Google's TPU architecture. It identifies Mike Gunter as co-founder and CTO. I treat the claims as an attributed company biography, and omit exact earlier employment years and departure timing not established by that page.", outlook: "I am watching whether the company's chip design becomes a dependable customer system with measured throughput, power consumption and software support. I could not retrieve a readable original record for the supplied later financing amount, so I omit the figure and an implied first-silicon delivery date. Product delivery will be more useful evidence than a speculative private-company price." },
  40: { education: "I have not confirmed the supplied degree award years against readable university records, so I omit those years.", career: "[Le Monde Informatique's June 2025 report](https://www.lemondeinformatique.fr/actualites/lireamp-telex-amd-renforce-son-pole-ia-avec-l-equipe-lamini-outset-leve-17-m$-pour-son-ia-intervieweuse-rappel-d-1-1-million-de-batteries-externes-anker-97133.html) says AMD brought in Lamini team members including CEO Sharon Zhou. AMD's [2026 Advancing AI event](https://www.amd.com/en/corporate/events/advancing-ai.html) supplies a later company record of her work. I do not treat the team move as proof that AMD acquired all of Lamini or assign undisclosed deal proceeds to Zhou." },
  21: { hook: "Joelle Pineau's [McGill biography](https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~jpineau/) connects academic machine learning with research leadership at Cohere. Her work makes reproducibility and deployment part of the same business question.", career: "Her [university biography](https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~jpineau/) documents work in reinforcement learning and planning under uncertainty. Her research leadership path includes Meta's FAIR organization and a later role at Cohere. I use the dated institutional biographies for those appointments rather than treating a past lab title as her current job. The combination matters because enterprise AI needs both credible evaluations and systems that continue to work on customer tasks.", outlook: "I am watching whether enterprise and sovereign AI deployments turn research progress into renewals and measurable workflow improvements. That company structure is examined separately in [who owns Cohere](https://visionarytalks.com/who-owns-cohere/); a pending transaction is not proof of completed ownership or of an individual's stake." },
  22: { hook: "Woosuk Kwon co-created vLLM, whose [open repository](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm) exposes the inference engine developers can test. Commercializing that work through Inferact creates a second question: what customers will pay for around an open project.", education: "His research biographies describe Seoul National University studies and doctoral research at Berkeley. I have not confirmed a completed Berkeley PhD award, and do not infer one from the project or company launch.", career: "The [vLLM repository](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm) provides the code and project record behind his inference work, including PagedAttention. [SiliconANGLE's January 22, 2026 report](https://siliconangle.com/2026/01/22/inferact-launches-150m-funding-commercialize-vllm/) describes the subsequent Inferact launch. I omit an aggregator's precise employment dates and volatile star counts: a repository's popularity is different from production usage and paid demand." },
  23: { hook: "Ying Sheng's SGLang work connects an open inference engine to RadixArk, a company commercializing AI infrastructure. The [SGLang repository](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang) offers a direct technical record to inspect.", education: "I have not confirmed the supplied degree award dates against university records, so I do not publish an aggregator's education chronology.", career: "[TechCrunch's January 2026 report](https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/21/sources-project-sglang-spins-out-as-radixark-with-400m-valuation-as-inference-market-explodes) connects the SGLang spinout to Sheng's earlier inference work. The repository and paper record show the technical project; the financing report describes the later business. I leave out precise internship dates that I could not establish from original records.", outlook: "[Pulse 2.0's May 5, 2026 account](https://pulse2.com/radixark-launches-with-100-million-in-seed-funding-to-democratize-frontier-ai-infrastructure/) reports a $100 million seed at a $400 million post-money valuation, led by Accel. I am watching whether hosted infrastructure offers enough operational value to win paid customers while preserving the portability that draws developers to an open engine." },
  24: { hook: "Georgi Gerganov's [llama.cpp](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp) is a direct route to running language models locally. Its economics differ from a cloud API: the user supplies the device and trades hardware constraints against privacy, latency and recurring cost.", education: "I could not confirm the supplied medical-physics degree against an original university record.", career: "[Hugging Face's February 20, 2026 announcement](https://huggingface.co/blog/ggml-joins-hf) says Gerganov and the ggml team joined it to support local AI. It says the team retains technical autonomy and dedicates its work to llama.cpp. The release does not disclose transaction terms, so I do not estimate a deal price or personal proceeds. The public repository makes the supported hardware and implementation visible to developers.", outlook: "The announcement sets out easier model conversion and packaging as goals. I am watching whether that reduces the effort required to run new open models on ordinary devices. A promised near-single-click conversion path is a roadmap, while a working release and reproducible performance are evidence that the goal has been delivered." },
  26: { hook: "Daniel Han's [Y Combinator biography](https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/unsloth-ai) credits him with finding and fixing more than 20 bugs in open models. That is a founder's attributed account, not an independent audit of every fix.", career: "The same YC record identifies NVIDIA algorithm work, the Hyperlearn package, co-founder Michael Han, a 2023 founding date and the Summer 2024 batch. Unsloth's [repository](https://github.com/unslothai/unsloth) supplies an inspectable record of its fine-tuning and reinforcement-learning tools. I leave out an aggregator's small seed amount and compare performance claims against specific workloads instead of treating one speedup as universal.", outlook: "I am watching whether hand-tuned kernels make custom models practical on cheaper hardware while preserving output quality. The economic benefit needs to survive a matched comparison: the same model, data, quality target and hardware. Downloads and repository stars show interest, but are not a measure of customer revenue or dependable savings." },
  27: { hook: "Omar Khattab's [DSPy repository](https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy) treats language-model pipelines as programs that can be optimized against examples. That shifts the problem from writing one clever prompt to maintaining a repeatable system.", education: "[MIT's faculty biography](https://www.eecs.mit.edu/people/omar-khattab/) records his academic background and appointment. I omit precise award months and dates not established in the opened institutional record.", career: "The MIT profile identifies his work in AI systems, while [DSPy's code](https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy) provides the implementation developers can use and test. The research connects retrieval and language models with a programming approach to compound systems. I do not infer adoption across all enterprise stacks from repository popularity or assign sole credit for a project with multiple contributors." },
  28: { hook: "Lysandre Debut co-authored [Hugging Face's December 2025 Transformers v5 announcement](https://huggingface.co/blog/transformers-v5). Its model definitions sit upstream of tools for training and inference, making compatibility work commercially important.", career: "The [PyTorch Foundation biography](https://pytorch.org/?p=1098) describes his open-source leadership at Hugging Face. The v5 release post names him among its authors and reports over 400 supported model architectures, up from 40 at the earlier v4 release. Those are company-reported ecosystem figures. The December 1 post announced a release candidate, v5.0.0rc-0, rather than establishing the date of every later stable release.", outlook: "I am watching whether simpler model definitions let downstream inference engines support new architectures faster without incompatible defaults. Hugging Face's [February 2026 ggml announcement](https://huggingface.co/blog/ggml-joins-hf) describes a closer path from Transformers definitions to llama.cpp; it is a roadmap that still needs working integrations." },
  29: { hook: "Erik Bernhardsson's [own biography](https://erikbern.com/about) traces Annoy and Spotify recommendation systems to his later infrastructure company, Modal. That is a useful career connection between building machine-learning systems and selling the compute they need." },
  30: { career: "Her [TWIML biography](https://twimlai.com/network/lin-qiao/) describes earlier work at IBM and LinkedIn and leadership of Meta's AI frameworks organization. The later Fireworks financing announcement documents the company she co-founded. I leave out precise employment months and team-size estimates not established by that institutional account. The progression connects model-development infrastructure with the later problem of serving specialized models to customers." },
  11: { hook: "Liam Fedus moved from language-model research to a company trying to automate scientific experiments. [Andreessen Horowitz's September 2025 announcement](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/) describes Periodic Labs as a combination of AI models and physical laboratories.", education: "I have not established his degree award dates from primary university records, so I leave those dates out.", career: "[OpenAI's o1 credits](https://openai.com/openai-o1-contributions/) name Fedus in reasoning-research leadership. The [Periodic Labs founding announcement](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-periodic-labs/) identifies the subsequent company and its plan to connect AI with physical experimentation. These records document two distinct stages: improving language-model systems, then building infrastructure that can generate experimental data instead of relying only on existing text. They do not establish the full chronology of earlier employment or a personal ownership percentage.", outlook: "The same investor announcement records a $300 million founding round; it does not disclose a valuation. I am watching whether automated experimentation produces reproducible materials results. Later fundraising discussions are not a closed round, and company capital raised is not Fedus's personal wealth." },
  12: { hook: "[Reflection's October 5, 2026 announcement](https://reflection.ai/blog/introducing-beam) describes Beam as a mixture-of-experts model with 501 billion total and 23 billion active parameters. Those are company-reported specifications, not an independent performance audit.", career: "His [personal biography](https://www.mishalaskin.com/) records theoretical-physics doctoral work, a Berkeley postdoc and a Y Combinator-backed startup. [NVIDIA's March 2026 coalition announcement](https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-launches-nemotron-coalition-of-leading-global-ai-labs-to-advance-open-frontier-models) identifies him as Reflection's co-founder and CEO. The move connects reinforcement-learning research with a company building frontier models; it does not prove that announced benchmarks translate into dependable production workloads.", outlook: "I am watching whether developers can download the promised weights and reproduce Beam's reported results at a usable serving cost. The model announcement describes a planned weights release later in October; announcing an open-weight model and delivering the artifact are separate milestones. I omit a later private valuation that I could not establish from a readable original record." },
  13: { role: "MiMo team lead, September 2026 report", organization: "Xiaomi MiMo", hook: "[TechNode's September 2026 report](https://technode.com/2026/09/29/xiaomi-promotes-mimo-team-lead-luo-fuli-to-its-top-job-level/) says Xiaomi promoted Luo Fuli, its MiMo team lead, to level 22. The report attributes the rank and September 22 internal announcement to LatePost, rather than a public company filing.", education: "A [Jiemian profile](https://www.jiemian.com/article/12362237.html) reports studies at Beijing Normal University and Peking University. I have not established degree award dates from university records.", career: "The [MiMo-V2-Flash technical report](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.02780), submitted in January 2026, includes Fuli Luo among the Xiaomi LLM-Core Team authors. That gives a research artifact to inspect alongside the later leadership report. I distinguish a named contribution from sole authorship and leave out uncertain joining dates and private compensation stories.", outlook: "I am watching whether Xiaomi can turn model research into reliable features across devices while retaining efficient inference. The technical report and deployed products are stronger tests than a promotion headline: an important rank is an organizational signal, but the business case depends on quality, latency and the cost of serving real users." },
  14: { role: "Chief AI scientist, December 2025 report", organization: "Tencent", hook: "[SCMP's December 17, 2025 report](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/tencent-restructures-ai-operations-promotes-093000045.html) identifies Yao Shunyu as Tencent's chief AI scientist under the CEO's office, reporting to president Martin Lau. That is a dated reporting record, not a continuously updated employment register.", education: "I could not verify the complete supplied degree chronology against readable university award records in this review.", career: "His [Reflexion research paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366) establishes a contribution to agents that use verbal feedback. The later Tencent report says he came from OpenAI and was also assigned leadership of LLM development and AI infrastructure. The distinction matters: a research paper shows a method, while an organizational appointment creates responsibility for training and inference platforms. I omit unconfirmed internship dates, competition awards and product-credit claims.", outlook: "I am watching whether agents use feedback to correct mistakes rather than merely elaborate them. At a large consumer technology company, the commercial test also includes deployment cost and consistency across user contexts. A stronger model benchmark alone does not show that feedback has become reliable memory in a working product." },
  15: { role: "Founder, August 2026 announcement", organization: "Pragmatik Labs; former Qwen lead", hook: "[The Standard's August 2026 report](https://www.thestandard.com.hk/finance/article/339744/Former-Alibaba-Qwen-AI-model-leader-founds-new-AI-company-with-funding-from-venture-giants) describes former Qwen leader Lin Junyang announcing Pragmatik Labs, five months after his departure from Alibaba.", education: "I have not confirmed his degree award year against a primary university record, so I omit the supplied date and unverified biographical details.", career: "The [Qwen2 technical report](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.10671) includes Junyang Lin in the team behind the open-weight model family. His later startup announcement marks a move from a large technology company to a venture pursuing agents across digital and physical worlds. The Standard names HSG and Gaorong as co-leading the funding and Tencent among the supporters. Paper authorship and a startup announcement establish different kinds of evidence; neither establishes personal wealth.", outlook: "The Standard reports funding worth hundreds of millions of dollars, including $100 million each from HSG and Gaorong and $20 million from Tencent. I attribute those amounts to the report rather than an audited cap table. I am watching whether the new venture releases models or products that outside developers can evaluate." },
  16: { career: "CMU's Language Technologies Institute records his doctorate in 2019. The [Kimi K2 technical report](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.20534) provides an inspectable model artifact from the company associated with his later work. That connection between research and commercial models is the useful thread here. I leave out unverified Kimi K3 rankings, parameter counts and IPO claims rather than treating aggregator headlines as original records." },
  17: { hook: "Chelsea Finn connects meta-learning research with general-purpose robotics. Her [Berkeley dissertation record](https://www2.eecs.berkeley.edu/Pubs/TechRpts/2018/EECS-2018-105.html) is titled Learning to Learn with Gradients; her Stanford biography identifies her as a Physical Intelligence co-founder.", career: "Her [Stanford biography](https://ai.stanford.edu/~cbfinn/) identifies an assistant professorship in computer science and electrical engineering and describes the IRIS lab's work on intelligence through robotic interaction. Her Berkeley dissertation, dated August 2018, documents the earlier meta-learning research. Together, those records trace the connection from learning algorithms to systems that must act in physical environments, where training data and evaluation are harder to collect than ordinary text.", outlook: "I am watching whether generalist robot policies transfer to unfamiliar tasks without substantial hidden setup work. Demonstrations should show success rates, failures and the labor needed to prepare a task. I omit later financing talks from this entry because the accessible intermediary source does not establish an original closing record." },
  18: { career: "Her [academic homepage](https://sherryy.github.io/) describes research in world models, agents, robotics and AI for science, following a Stanford postdoc. Its news record includes CrystalReasoner in May 2026. [Harvard Kempner's February 2026 event](https://kempnerinstitute.harvard.edu/events/sherry-yang) independently identifies her as an NYU assistant professor and Google DeepMind research scientist. I use those records for the affiliations rather than extrapolating employment dates from an aggregator.", outlook: "I am watching whether world models improve learning in environments where real experiments are expensive. Her research connects simulation to robotics and scientific discovery; the valuable test is whether a policy trained in that cheaper environment works under the physical or scientific constraints of its intended application." },
  19: { hook: "Levent Alpöge and Tristan Buckmaster's AI-assisted mathematics work became part of a September 2026 dispute over attribution. [OpenAI's account](https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/) acknowledges the priority of their forced-Euler work; that acknowledgment is separate from independent acceptance of a proof.", education: "I have not established the supplied degree and scholarship chronology from readable primary award records, so I leave it out.", career: "The [September 2026 university account](https://rits.shanghai.nyu.edu/ai/openai-navier-stokes-proof-credit-dispute/) describes a dispute over related fluid-equation results. OpenAI's original post identifies Alpöge as an Anthropic employee and acknowledges work with Buckmaster. I distinguish each party's account, a formal checking artifact and independent mathematical acceptance. Those records do not justify attributing every result in the dispute to one person or saying that a prize was awarded." },
  20: { role: "Researcher and founder", organization: "Adaption Labs", outlook: "Her [current biography](https://www.sarahooker.me/) describes Adaption's focus on efficient systems that continually learn. I am watching whether that approach maintains quality as data changes, without pushing unreported costs into retraining or evaluation. I omit a seed amount that I could not verify from an original financing announcement." },
  1: { hook: "[The Decoder's July 2025 report](https://the-decoder.com/openai-claims-a-breakthrough-in-llm-reasoning-on-complex-math-problems/) attributes to Alexander Wei's OpenAI team an experimental model scoring 35 of 42 points on the International Mathematical Olympiad, solving five of six problems. It describes a company-reported result, not an official medal awarded to the model." },
  2: { role: "Founder; former OpenAI reasoning leader", organization: "Core Automation", hook: "Jerry Tworek's name appears beside overall reasoning leadership in OpenAI's o1 credits. After leaving OpenAI in January 2026, he launched Core Automation, shifting from a large lab to a startup focused on automating research.", education: "I could not establish his degree or graduation year from a primary education record.", career: "OpenAI's [o1 contribution record](https://openai.com/openai-o1-contributions/) names Tworek in overall reasoning leadership. [Business Today's January 7, 2026 report](https://www.businesstoday.in/amp/technology/news/story/openai-reasoning-chief-jerry-tworek-quits-after-7-years-heres-why-509827-2026-01-07) describes his departure after seven years and his work on Codex and post-training. [The Decoder's April launch report](https://the-decoder.com/ex-openai-researcher-jerry-tworek-launches-core-automation-to-build-the-most-automated-ai-lab-in-the-world/) describes a new lab pursuing learning algorithms beyond pretraining and reinforcement learning.", outlook: "[The Decoder reported in May 2026](https://the-decoder.com/ai-money-keeps-flowing-as-deepseek-plans-record-raise-and-core-automation-quadruples-valuation-in-weeks/) that Core Automation had raised $100 million at a $1 billion valuation and was targeting about $4 billion in another round. The latter was fundraising talk, not a confirmed closing. I am watching whether the lab turns that capital into repeatable improvements in model learning." },
  3: { education: "[Dartmouth's May 2023 alumni report](https://home.dartmouth.edu/news/2023/05/recent-alumnus-lectures-advances-ai) identifies him as a 2020 graduate. I do not infer an additional degree from his research appointments." },
  4: { role: "Researcher; reported Meta hire, June 2025", organization: "Meta hiring report / OpenAI o1 record", hook: "Hongyu Ren appears among the foundational reasoning contributors in [OpenAI's o1 credit record](https://openai.com/openai-o1-contributions/). [Fortune reported in June 2025](https://fortune.com/2025/06/28/meta-four-openai-researchers-superintelligence-team-ai-talent-competition) that Meta hired him alongside three other OpenAI researchers.", education: "I could not retrieve a readable current primary education record during this review, so I do not publish the supplied degree dates.", career: "The o1 record establishes a contribution to reasoning research, rather than sole authorship of a model. Fortune's June 28, 2025 report names Ren, Jiahui Yu, Shuchao Bi and Shengjia Zhao in the same hiring group. Those are dated records: the OpenAI credits describe earlier work and the hiring report describes a later move. Neither gives a verified chronology of internships or assigns him responsibility for every subsequent model.", outlook: "I am watching the economics of efficient reasoning: whether better answers can be delivered with less inference compute, and whether evaluations separate accuracy from persuasive explanations. A hiring headline is useful context, but reproducible evidence of better reasoning per dollar would be the stronger investment signal." },
  5: { career: "His [February 28, 2025 announcement](https://x.com/_sholtodouglas/status/1895610467818901609) says he joined Anthropic at the start of that month after Google DeepMind. The [May 2025 Dwarkesh interview](https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/sholto-trenton-2) describes his work scaling reinforcement learning at Anthropic. I use those first-person records for the move and research focus, rather than assigning him sole credit for Claude's capabilities." },
  6: { career: "His mathematics record traces a move from knot theory into AI interpretability. Anthropic's [On the Biology of a Large Language Model](https://transformer-circuits.pub/2025/attribution-graphs/biology.html) credits him as a core contributor and correspondence author on the March 2025 attribution-graph project. Graph construction, interventions and visualization are distinct responsibilities; that credit does not mean one researcher explained an entire model. His Anthropic start date remains unestablished." },
  7: { hook: "Julian Schrittwieser's research path runs from Go-playing systems to formal mathematics. His own AlphaProof account ties the project to work at Google DeepMind from early 2022 to July 2024.", career: "[MIT Technology Review's profile](https://www.technologyreview.com/innovator/julian-schrittwieser/) describes his engineering work on AlphaGo and AlphaGo Zero. His [November 2025 AlphaProof account](https://www.julian.ac/index.html) says the system discovered Lean proofs for the 2024 International Mathematical Olympiad at silver-medal performance, with a paper published in Nature. It explicitly dates the work to his earlier Google DeepMind tenure. I retain that chronology rather than treating a later publication date as proof of a current employer.", outlook: "His [personal essays](https://www.julian.ac/index.html) discuss rising AI task lengths and the implications for work. I am watching whether reinforcement learning transfers from tightly specified games and formal proofs to tasks with ambiguous goals, incomplete feedback and a real cost when an agent makes a mistake." },
  8: { hook: "Neel Nanda's [own biography](https://www.neelnanda.io/about) says he runs Google DeepMind's mechanistic interpretability team and mentors researchers through MATS. His route into that work began in pure mathematics and included quant-finance internships.", career: "The same biography records internships at Jane Street and Jump Trading, followed by AI safety work at the Future of Humanity Institute, DeepMind and CHAI. He then did independent interpretability research and worked at Anthropic under Chris Olah before leading the Google DeepMind team. He describes the team's job as reverse engineering the algorithms and structures learned by neural networks; that is a concrete research mandate rather than a claim that every model behavior is already understood.", outlook: "I am watching whether interpretability tools become useful in deployment decisions: predicting failures, identifying a mechanism and testing whether an intervention changes it. His mentoring work also makes the supply of trained researchers part of the story; a technique that only its original authors can operate has a limited auditing market." },
  9: { hook: "Jack Parker-Holder works on Genie, Google DeepMind's world-model research. [TechCrunch's August 2025 report](https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/05/deepmind-reveals-genie-3-a-world-model-that-could-be-the-key-to-reaching-agi) attributes 720p generation at 24 frames per second to Genie 3, then a research preview rather than a generally available product.", outlook: "I am watching whether simulated environments improve training for embodied agents and robotics. [His 2026 interview with Heise](https://heise.de/-11304842) discusses Street View integration and physical-world uses. The financial question is whether useful training scenarios can be produced more cheaply without sacrificing the accuracy needed for transfer to real environments." },
  10: { role: "Research writer; reported OpenAI return, July 2026", organization: "Lil'Log / reported OpenAI role", hook: "Lilian Weng's [Lil'Log](https://lilianweng.github.io/) turns technical research into readable notes on reinforcement learning, hallucinations and model behavior. [July 2026 reporting](https://americanbazaaronline.com/2026/07/30/weng-leaves-thinking-machines-to-rejoin-openai-485496/) says she left Thinking Machines Lab and returned to OpenAI to work on recursive self-improvement.", education: "An [Ifanr profile](https://www.ifanr.com/1673220) reports Peking University studies beginning in 2005 and subsequent doctoral study at Indiana University Bloomington. I have not confirmed the degree award year from a university record.", career: "The July departure reporting describes earlier OpenAI robotics and safety work, followed by co-founding Thinking Machines Lab. I treat that as an attributed career account, not a primary employment register. Her own blog dates its learning notes to 2017 and provides a direct record of her writing. Research on reward hacking, hallucinations and test-time compute connects her public explanations to practical questions about model reliability.", outlook: "I am watching whether AI-assisted model development yields measurable improvements against a fixed baseline. Faster research cycles would matter economically, but a claim of self-improvement needs a clear account of what was automated, what humans supplied and whether the gains replicate outside the team's own evaluation." }
};
Object.assign(module.exports[21], {
  education: "Her [McGill biography](https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~jpineau/) records a Waterloo engineering BASc and Carnegie Mellon robotics MSc and PhD. I omit precise award years because that readable record does not state them.",
  hook: "[Cohere's August 2025 announcement](https://cohere.com/blog/august-2025-funding-round) appoints Joelle Pineau chief AI officer after leading Meta's FAIR organization. Her academic and industry path makes reproducibility part of the enterprise-AI question.",
  career: "Her [McGill biography](https://www.cs.mcgill.ca/~jpineau/) describes planning under uncertainty and applications in robotics, healthcare and conversational agents. It records FAIR leadership from 2017 to 2025 and a current part-time leave from McGill. Cohere's later appointment announcement establishes the new company role. I keep those dated records separate instead of treating an older university page as a full current employment register."
});
